<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0602">Example <span data-tag="0602">8.5.3.14</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category, let $F: \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{Idem}) \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an idempotent in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $e: X \rightarrow X$ be the morphism in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ given by the image of the nondegenerate edge of $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{Idem})$. Suppose that $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ is finite-dimensional when viewed as a simplicial set. Then, for $n &gt; \dim (\operatorname{\mathcal{C}})$, the functor $F$ carries the unique nondegenerate $n$-simplex $\sigma _{n}$ of $\operatorname{N}_{\bullet }(\operatorname{Idem})$ to a degenerate $n$-simplex of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. Since the composition $(F \circ \sigma _{n}): \Delta ^ n \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ carries every nondegenerate edge of $\Delta ^ n$ to the morphism $e$, we conclude that $e = \operatorname{id}_{X}$. Invoking Example <a data-tag="0601" href="/tag/0601">8.5.3.13</a>, we conclude that $F$ is isomorphic to the constant functor $\underline{X}$; in particular, it is a split idempotent. </p>
